but more people trust the IRS than they  did Facebook with protecting their  privacy  i'm paul dietrich with reason TV and  today we're talking with recent  foundation polling director emily Eakins  there's a new reason drupe poll out  today that deals in part with NSA  surveillance thanks for joining us Emily  thanks for having me with the revelation  in The Guardian The Washington Post's  and other publications about the NSA  looking after people's internet data  their phone call records what did you  find so we asked them to choose if they  saw the program primarily as a means to  fight terrorism or primarily a violation  of privacy and a majority of Americans  said it was a violation of privacy so  while most Americans do you agree that  it's a violation of privacy independents  are the most likely group to say it's a  violation of privacy particularly  independents who lean Republican and  this is something we often find in our  polls that a lot of libertarians are  found in the group that identifies is as  independent but they lean Republican do  you think libertarians are going to be  upset with that kind of distinction  perhaps but to that point a lot of civil  libertarians are also found in the  independent group but they lean left  now our Americans up in arms about all  of this not exactly we asked Americans  if they thought the NSA surveillance  program had impacted their trust in  government only a third said that it had  decreased their trust in government  whereas a majority said it really made  no difference either way you also asked  about Edward Snowden people keep asking  if he's a traitor or a patriot what did  you find  Americans are split about a third say  he's a patriot a third say he's a  traitor and the rest don't really have  an opinion partisans are more likely to  say he's a traitor and independents are  more likely to say that he is a patriot  young people in particular were twice as  likely as older Americans to say that  he's a patriot you found something  interesting when you looked at a  Facebook and the Internal Revenue  Service the IRS well we were interested  in what and who Americans trusted more  in protecting their privacy Facebook the  IRS Google the NSA and what we found  might surprise you  now most people don't trust any of those  groups in protecting their privacy but  more people trust the eye  ress than they did Facebook with  protecting their privacy but we have all  these things associated with the IRS  that they get into politics that they  they use their power to go after  citizens they still trust IRS over  Facebook  well to be clear about two-thirds don't  trust the IRS with their privacy  it's just that three-fourths don't trust  Facebook and it probably has something  to do with the fact that people upload  pictures personal information to  Facebook and so they invest a lot of  trust in Facebook protecting their  privacy the Obama administration has  said that they only use the NSA data to  fight terrorism to go after terrorists  but there's a chance that it could be  shared with other government departments  other government entities are people  afraid of this at first glance Americans  oppose the NSA sharing the secret data  it collects on millions of Americans for  anything other than combating terrorism  however we asked about a few particular  circumstances and we found that  attitudes changed now first about  two-thirds of Americans say that the NSA  should not share secret data with the  IRS but they would favor sharing it with  law enforcement agencies looking for a  missing child we also asked about  whether the NSA should be allowed to  share its data with the Drug Enforcement  Administration in that situation  Americans were split right down the  middle with younger people more likely  to say it should not share it with the  DEA and older people saying that it  should so what we learned from this is  that while Americans don't want the data  to be used for things other than you  know combating terrorism when you give  them particular circumstances especially  those that are more emotionally charged  then attitudes change and they're more  open to sharing the data  the reason root pool covers a lot of  different topics what else did you ask  about in this poll we also asked  Americans about what they think about  government spending the debt ceiling the  implementation of the new health care  law the Detroit bankruptcy among others  the full poll results will be available  at recent calm forward slash Paul thank  you very much Emily Eakins thanks for  having me for recent TV i'm paul  dietrich  you
 

